  "intro": "The U.S. exported **$18.02 billion** of Chapter 08 goods in 2025, up **9.8%** from 2024. Tree nuts drive the chapter: heading 0802 is **59%** of it, and almonds alone (0802.11 and 0802.12) are **$6.08 billion**, about a third, ahead of in-shell pistachios ($2.33 billion) and shelled walnuts ($993 million). Canada is the largest buyer (**24%**), mostly of fresh fruit, followed by India (8%, almost all almonds) and Mexico (8%).\n\nFor most of the trade, tariffs are low. Canada and Mexico take qualifying U.S. fruit and nuts duty-free under USMCA, South Korea mostly duty-free under KORUS, and since July 1, 2026 the EU lets U.S. tree nuts in duty-free within a 500,000-tonne yearly quota. The costly markets are India, which charges a per-kilo duty on almonds and 50% on apples, and China, which adds a 15% counter-tariff from 2018 and an extra 10% on every U.S. good to its normal rate; U.S. sales to China fell **83%** to $162 million in 2025. For fresh fruit, what usually decides access is the buyer’s plant-health rules and the USDA phytosanitary certificate, not the tariff.",
